  "slug": "22q11-deletion-syndrome-c722",
  "summary": "Recent preprints link variable schizophrenia penetrance in 22q11.2 deletion to neuronal mitophagy, barrier and oligodendrocyte differences.",
  "synthesis": "Three of the six preprints examine 22q11.2 deletion carriers or deletion models, and the dominant thread is cellular compartments associated with variable schizophrenia penetrance. In iPSC-derived neurons from deletion carriers, schizophrenia is associated with impaired mitophagy and lysosomal acidity, a defect described as reversible. In iPSC-derived blood-brain barrier cells from carriers, metabolic and structural differences are linked to penetrance. In a 22q11.2 deletion mouse model, prefrontal oligodendrocyte development is altered, with more immature and mature oligodendrocytes and reduced myelination at P28, although this study does not address penetrance. The new element is the association of penetrance with distinct cellular states across neuronal and barrier compartments, alongside glial changes in a deletion model. The remaining three studies use cntnap2-/- autism, PLX5622 Purkinje cell degeneration and LKB1 granule cell models and are not attributed to the deletion.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-25"
}
